{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7EQSc7H7kx8fktQrTDW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written this to about 390 words, which is roughly 2 minutes 45 seconds at a calm pace, so you have some room to spare. It opens with traffic because you're the person in the room who lives with it, and that makes the traffic signal argument hard to dismiss.\n\n<final_document>\nGood evening, Commissioners. My name is Margaret Shaw. I've lived on Alder Road for 19 years, two blocks from St. Luke's Lutheran, and I'm a member of that church. So I'm here tonight as a neighbor and as a member, and I'm asking you to approve this permit, with a traffic signal at Alder and 4th as a condition.\n\nI'll start with traffic, because I live with it. Some mornings around eight, cars on Alder already back up past my house. So when the traffic study said this project would add 310 trips a day and drop Alder and 4th from a C to a D at rush hour, I paid attention.\n\nBut the same study says something else. Cascade Housing Partners has offered to pay for a signal at that intersection, and with the signal, the intersection stays at a C. That's why I'm asking you to make the signal a condition of approval, not just a promise. If it's written into the permit, the neighborhood gets the fix along with the homes.\n\nOn parking: the plan has 72 spaces for 64 apartments. That's more than one space for every home.\n\nNow, why these homes matter. The county's own housing report says we are short about 2,100 affordable homes. Right now, 412 households are on the housing authority's waiting list, and the average wait is three years. Three years. Every one of these 64 apartments would rent to households earning under 60 percent of the area median income. Those are the kinds of households on that list.\n\nAnd the land is already there. The church's back lot is 1.8 acres, and it sits empty six days a week. Nothing has to be torn down and no land has to be bought. We have a paved lot that sits unused most of the week, and hundreds of households waiting for a place to live.\n\nI know change on our street can be hard to picture. But I would rather have 64 more households as neighbors on Alder than a parking lot that's empty six days out of seven.\n\nPlease approve the permit, with the traffic signal at Alder and 4th as a condition. Thank you.\n</final_document>\n\nA few notes for tonight:\n\n- **The permit's name:** I just called it \"the permit.\" If the agenda lists a specific name, like \"conditional use permit,\" use that name in the first and last paragraphs. The clerk can tell you before the meeting starts.\n- **If you speak after opponents:** Listen for any objection you can answer in one sentence from these facts, and swap it in for the parking paragraph. Parking is the easiest part to cut.\n- **If time runs short:** Cut the second-to-last paragraph (\"I know change on our street…\"). Always keep the last line, because it's your actual request.\n- Print it in large type and practice it once out loud with a timer."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":880,"cache_read_input_tokens":9789,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":880},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"969978e2-8455-4aea-8781-6d0f9e41159b","uuid":"b33fa853-7133-4572-a261-9329da3bcd94","timestamp":"2026-11-16T20:24:05.588Z","request_id":"req_011Ch7EQSQDAoadUisNSDbxR"}
